## The door: how clients get in

Every request to a protected route carries a **client token**:

```bash
curl -sS -H 'Authorization: Bearer <client token>' \
     -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
     -d '{}' http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/…
```

A client token is issued per client, on the dashboard's **Clients** page
(a name, one click) or, for a service without a dashboard, with
`chassis clients` on the machine that runs it. The token is shown once and
can be revealed, copied, re-issued or revoked at any time; revoking locks
the client out the same second. A request without a valid token is
answered with HTTP 401 and a JSON body that says what to send.


Two secrets belong to the operator, not to clients, and live only in the
environment file (`{{ env_file }}`), never in a flag or a config file:

- `{{ prefix }}_TOKEN` — the **admin token**: it logs you in on `/login`
  and also works as a bearer for scripts run by the operator.
- `{{ prefix }}_SECRET_KEY` — the **secret key** (32 bytes, hex) that seals
  the stores on disk: clients and sessions{% if "passkeys" in features %}, and passkeys{% endif %}.

Both come from one command, run on a terminal (it refuses a pipe, so a
secret never lands in a log):

```bash
{{ name }} gen-secret
```

Paste the two lines it prints into `{{ env_file }}`. Without both, a
service with a dashboard refuses to start and `--check` tells you so.
{%- else %}

## The door: how clients get in

The kit's door — client tokens, the admin token, the sealed client store —
comes with the `dashboard` feature, which {{ name }} is not built with. The
kit adds no authentication of its own here: every route this binary serves
is one the project registered itself, and whatever protects it is the
project's own. `{{ prefix }}_SECRET_KEY` and `{{ prefix }}_TOKEN` are not
read, and `gen-secret` is not part of this binary.

To get a token FROM another service built on the kit (a hub this service
calls), use `chassis clients issue <name> --url <base> --token-env <VAR>`
on the machine that runs it; that command talks to the other service's
API, not to this one.
{%- endif %}

