## Who is calling: `Caller`

Every handler the project writes receives a `Caller`, which answers who sent
this request rather than what kind of thing it is. It has two shapes: the
admin, who logged in on the dashboard or sent the admin token, and a client,
one of the rows on the Clients page. So "Clients" is the name of the thing
that registers, and `Caller::Client` is that thing arriving at a route.
{% endif %}

## Configuration knobs

The project's own settings live in the same config file and are none of the
kit's business: `app.project_config()` hands the project everything the kit
does not own, table sections included.

Every knob answers to three names: a flag, an environment variable and a
key in the config file (`{{ state_dir }}/config.toml`). Precedence is
**flag > env > file > default**. Secret knobs have no flag and are refused
from the file. The **Feature** column says which kit feature reads the
knob; a knob of a feature this binary was built without is accepted and
ignored.

{{ knobs_table | trim }}

## Where things live

- **State**: `{{ prefix }}_STATE_DIR`, default `{{ state_dir }}`. The
  config file{% if "dashboard" in features %}, the sealed stores{% endif %} and the update state all live under
  this one directory; nothing is written anywhere else.
- **Environment file**: `{{ env_file }}`, read by the systemd unit
  (`EnvironmentFile=`). It holds {% if "dashboard" in features %}the two secrets and {% endif %}any knob you set as
  an environment variable.
{%- if "self-update" in features %}
- **Pre-update copies**: `{{ state_dir }}-pre-update/`, beside the state
  root, not under it.
{%- endif %}

